        "problem": "If $R=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}]$, then ${R}^{5}=$",
        "worked_solution": "Computing ${R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & =R\\cdot R \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${R}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $R$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${R}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{5} & ={R}^{2}\\cdot {R}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {R}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{R}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
